WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.913
Communication sounds soft, but it is testable and it shapes every bedside outcome.

2
00:00:06.913 --> 00:00:09.964
This chapter walks through four pieces.

3
00:00:09.964 --> 00:00:19.427
First, the difference between closed and open questions, and the interview strategy of starting broad and narrowing down.

4
00:00:19.427 --> 00:00:29.360
Second, the five levels of communication, from cliches to peak intimacy, and where the therapeutic relationship actually lives.

5
00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:38.667
Third, proxemics, the four distance zones, and why respiratory therapists live in the intimate space of their patients.

6
00:00:38.667 --> 00:00:45.550
And fourth, the three V's, which remind us that most of the message is not in the words.

7
00:00:46.967 --> 00:00:53.709
Closed questions produce short specific answers, often yes or no or a single number.

8
00:00:53.709 --> 00:01:01.816
They are useful when you need information quickly, especially from a patient in respiratory distress.

9
00:01:01.816 --> 00:01:11.609
Open questions are thought provoking and invite the patient to tell their story, surfacing things you did not know to ask.

10
00:01:11.609 --> 00:01:18.672
Both are appropriate, and the respiratory therapist's skill is knowing when to use each.

11
00:01:18.672 --> 00:01:29.428
Hess adds that open questions are the most valuable form during the initial assessment and almost always begin with what, why, or how.

12
00:01:29.428 --> 00:01:39.542
Avoid leading questions, which suggest the answer you want, and compound questions, where a yes leaves you asking yes to what.

13
00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:45.393
The teaching sequence is start broad, then narrow.

14
00:01:45.393 --> 00:01:50.527
Begin with an open question like tell me about your cough.

15
00:01:50.527 --> 00:01:59.467
Then move to focused closed questions: when did it start, is it productive, what color is the sputum.

16
00:01:59.467 --> 00:02:01.857
Clarify anything ambiguous.

17
00:02:01.857 --> 00:02:05.132
Avoid leading and compound questions.

18
00:02:05.132 --> 00:02:10.709
Use silence and facilitation to let the patient fill the space.

19
00:02:10.709 --> 00:02:13.276
Finally summarize to confirm.

20
00:02:13.276 --> 00:02:24.075
If you notice an inconsistency, the deck calls this respectfully identifying inconsistencies; Hess calls it confrontation.

21
00:02:24.075 --> 00:02:37.442
The spirit is the same: honestly point out a mismatch, for example you said your breathing was fine but I noticed your respiratory rate was quite high.

22
00:02:38.867 --> 00:02:44.858
The deck numbers levels from five most superficial down to one deepest.

23
00:02:44.858 --> 00:02:49.331
Level five is cliche conversation: how are you, fine.

24
00:02:49.331 --> 00:02:55.576
Level four is reporting facts, where most routine healthcare talk happens.

25
00:02:55.576 --> 00:03:03.171
Level three is personal ideas or judgments: I don't think these treatments are helping me.

26
00:03:03.171 --> 00:03:09.922
Level two is feelings and emotions: I am scared I will end up on the vent again.

27
00:03:09.922 --> 00:03:17.095
Level one is peak communication, intimate, reserved for close personal relationships.

28
00:03:17.095 --> 00:03:21.399
The therapeutic sweet spot is levels two and three.

29
00:03:21.399 --> 00:03:25.872
The chain to remember is rapport, trust, cooperation.

30
00:03:25.872 --> 00:03:34.142
Patients may self disclose at level two; the therapist responds therapeutically, not reciprocally.

31
00:03:35.567 --> 00:03:39.358
Proxemics is how distance shapes communication.

32
00:03:39.358 --> 00:03:42.181
The deck lists four zones, in feet.

33
00:03:42.181 --> 00:03:52.990
Intimate is zero to one and a half, personal is one and a half to four, social is four to twelve, and public is twelve to twenty five.

34
00:03:52.990 --> 00:04:05.494
The respiratory therapist works inside the intimate zone constantly: suctioning, auscultating, placing a BiPAP mask, drawing an ABG, performing trach care.

35
00:04:05.494 --> 00:04:11.302
That is a space patients normally reserve for people very close to them.

36
00:04:11.302 --> 00:04:20.094
The skill is what you do before you enter: explain and ask, I am going to listen to your lungs, is that okay?

37
00:04:20.094 --> 00:04:26.467
These are conceptual zones that vary with culture, relationship, and condition.

38
00:04:27.867 --> 00:04:30.548
The three V's split the message.

39
00:04:30.548 --> 00:04:34.068
Verbal, the words, is about seven percent.

40
00:04:34.068 --> 00:04:39.935
Vocal, meaning tone, volume, pitch, and rate, is thirty eight percent.

41
00:04:39.935 --> 00:04:47.896
Visual, meaning facial expression, gestures, posture, and body language, is fifty five percent.

42
00:04:47.896 --> 00:04:52.757
So about ninety three percent of the message is nonverbal.

43
00:04:52.757 --> 00:05:02.814
When verbal and nonverbal signals conflict, people lean on vocal and visual cues, especially for feelings and attitudes.

44
00:05:02.814 --> 00:05:10.943
The caveat: Mehrabian's percentages describe the communication of feelings when signals disagree.

45
00:05:10.943 --> 00:05:16.139
The numbers do not apply to a command like change PEEP to ten.

46
00:05:16.139 --> 00:05:22.592
Memorize seven thirty eight fifty five, and know it is an overgeneralization.

47
00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:30.781
A nurse hands you a patient who is working to breathe and cannot speak in full sentences.

48
00:05:30.781 --> 00:05:32.762
You need information fast.

49
00:05:32.762 --> 00:05:43.200
Which questioning type does the deck recommend as the primary tool at this moment, and name two question types to avoid in any interview.

50
00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:50.100
[6-second practice pause]

51
00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:52.164
Use closed questions.

52
00:05:52.164 --> 00:06:04.003
The deck specifically says closed questions are extremely useful when you need specific information quickly, especially with a patient in respiratory distress.

53
00:06:04.003 --> 00:06:18.150
The two question types to avoid are leading questions, which suggest the answer you want, and compound questions, which ask several things at once so you cannot tell what the yes applies to.

54
00:06:19.567 --> 00:06:23.100
You are placing a BiPAP mask on a new admit.

55
00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:34.585
Name the distance zone you are operating in, and name the percent of your communication that is now nonverbal according to the three V's model.

56
00:06:34.585 --> 00:06:37.717
Pause and answer before the next slide.

57
00:06:38.633 --> 00:06:44.633
[6-second practice pause]

58
00:06:45.133 --> 00:06:54.830
You are inside the intimate zone, zero to one and a half feet, a space the patient normally saves for close relationships.

59
00:06:54.830 --> 00:07:06.036
By the three V's, about ninety three percent of your message is nonverbal, split as thirty eight percent vocal and fifty five percent visual.

60
00:07:06.036 --> 00:07:12.394
So your tone, your face, and how you handle the mask matter more than the words.

61
00:07:12.394 --> 00:07:17.083
Explain, ask permission, and keep your face and voice calm.

62
00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:19.236
To recap.

63
00:07:19.236 --> 00:07:28.729
Open questions to start, closed questions to focus, avoid leading and compound questions, and use silence to listen.

64
00:07:28.729 --> 00:07:37.894
The five levels run from cliche down to peak; aim for the therapeutic sweet spot of feelings and personal ideas.

65
00:07:37.894 --> 00:07:53.115
Proxemics has four zones: intimate, personal, social, public, with boundaries at one and a half, four, twelve, and twenty five feet, and respiratory therapists live in the intimate zone.

66
00:07:53.115 --> 00:08:01.299
The three V's are seven, thirty eight, fifty five, so most of the message is tone and body language.

67
00:08:01.299 --> 00:08:07.600
Ask permission before you enter and keep verbal, vocal, and visual congruent.
